'' ===========================================================================
''  pnut-ts AUGS sign-extension defect -- minimal reproducer
''
''  SYMPTOM: an explicit `AUGS #v` whose operand has BIT 31 SET assembles as
''           AUGD.  Silently.  No warning, no error.
''
''  FULL GUIDE: defects/RECOGNISING-THE-DEFECT.md  (listing-file reading too)
''
''  HOW TO RECOGNISE IT, and this test does not depend on knowing what the
''  payload SHOULD be -- only on the opcode field:
''
''      take each emitted 32-bit long, look at BITS [27:23]
''          11110  = AUGS
''          11111  = AUGD
''      If the source says `augs` and bits [27:23] are 11111, that is the bug.
''
''  Equivalently, in hex: an AUGS long begins FF0..FF7, an AUGD long begins
''  FF8..FFF.  Every long below is CCCC=1111 (always), so the top byte is FF
''  in every case and only the next nibble separates the two.
''
''  MECHANISM (inferred, and it predicts every case below): the operand is
''  right-shifted 9 places ARITHMETICALLY -- the sign propagates -- and then
''  masked to 24 bits (0xFFFFFF) where the payload field is only 23 bits
''  (0x7FFFFF).  The stray bit 23 is the low bit of the opcode field, so a
''  negative operand turns 11110 into 11111.
''      emitted == 0xFF000000 | ((signed(v) >> 9) & 0xFFFFFF)
'' ===========================================================================

DAT
                org     0

'' ---- A.  THE DEFECT.  Bit 31 of the operand is set. -----------------------
''          pnut-ts 1.55.5 emits AUGD (bits[27:23] = 11111) for all three.

                augs    #$FFFFFFFF      ' emits FFFFFFFF  <-- AUGD, should be AUGS
                augs    #$80000000      ' emits FFC00000  <-- AUGD, should be AUGS
                augs    #$FFFFF123      ' emits FFFFFFF8  <-- AUGD, should be AUGS

'' ---- B.  CONTROL.  Bit 31 clear.  pnut-ts is correct here. ---------------
''          All three emit AUGS (bits[27:23] = 11110).

                augs    #$7FFFFFFF      ' emits FF3FFFFF      AUGS, correct
                augs    #$40000000      ' emits FF200000      AUGS, correct
                augs    #$00FFF123      ' emits FF007FF8      AUGS, correct

'' ---- C.  CONTROL.  The `##` auto-prefix path.  Correct even with bit 31. --
''          This is why ordinary code is unaffected: `##` does not go through
''          the defective path.  Two longs: the AUGS prefix, then the ADD.

                add     0-0,##$FFFFF123 ' emits FF7FFFF8      AUGS, correct
                                        '  then F1040123      the ADD

'' ---- D.  AUGD, same arithmetic, corruption invisible. --------------------
''          AUGD's opcode already has bit 23 set, so the stray bit changes
''          nothing observable.  Included so a comparison does not mistake
''          AUGD's correctness for the absence of the defect.

                augd    #$FFFFFFFF      ' emits FFFFFFFF      AUGD, correct
